Install order
=============

The webhook cannot be applied from this directory as it stands: the API server
needs a caBundle, and the serving Secret does not exist yet. Both come from one
offline command.

1. Issue the PKI. Writes ferrum-admission-tls.secret.yaml (mode 0600), ca.crt,
   ca.key (mode 0600), and validatingwebhookconfiguration.yaml rendered from
   the template:

       ferrumctl gen-webhook-pki --service ferrum-admission --namespace ferrum \
           --days 365 --out-dir deploy/admission

   It refuses to overwrite existing output. Neither the Secret, the rendered
   configuration, nor ca.key belongs in git. ca.key never reaches the cluster;
   it is what step 4 rotates with, so keep it offline and keep it. The
   .gitignore in this directory covers all four, and `ferrumctl lint-deploy`
   (FD023) fails on a PEM private key anywhere under the deploy tree — whoever
   holds ca.key can issue a leaf the applied webhook trusts, i.e. forge this
   webhook. Move ca.key out of the tree once step 2 is done; the lint keeps
   failing while it is there.

   The CA is issued for the longest lifetime the rule allows (398 days),
   whatever --days the serving certificate gets. That headroom is the only
   reason the leaf can be rotated later without touching the caBundle.

2. Apply the Secret, then the rest of this directory:

       kubectl apply -f deploy/namespace.yaml
       kubectl apply -f deploy/admission/ferrum-admission-tls.secret.yaml
       kubectl apply -f deploy/admission/serviceaccount.yaml \
                     -f deploy/admission/rbac.yaml \
                     -f deploy/admission/service.yaml \
                     -f deploy/admission/deployment.yaml

3. Apply the webhook configuration last: with failurePolicy=Fail it starts
   rejecting requests the moment it exists.

       kubectl apply -f deploy/admission/validatingwebhookconfiguration.yaml

Build features
==============

deployment.yaml passes --apiserver, so the image must be built with the
`apiserver` feature: without it the binary refuses that flag and exits. Drop
the flag only if you accept that every policy carrying a namespace or
ServiceAccount selector denies each Pod it selects.

Rotation
========

4. Before the serving certificate expires, reissue only the leaf, under the CA
   the cluster already trusts. The caBundle does not change, so the
   ValidatingWebhookConfiguration from step 3 stays applied untouched — nothing
   in the cluster is briefly without a webhook:

       mkdir deploy/admission/rotated
       ferrumctl gen-webhook-pki --service ferrum-admission --namespace ferrum \
           --days 90 --out-dir deploy/admission/rotated \
           --ca-cert deploy/admission/ca.crt --ca-key deploy/admission/ca.key \
           --webhook-config <the validatingwebhookconfiguration.yaml applied in step 3>

       kubectl apply -f deploy/admission/rotated/ferrum-admission-tls.secret.yaml

   The command writes the Secret and nothing else: no new CA, no re-rendered
   configuration. It refuses if --days would outlive the CA (a leaf may not
   outlive its issuer, and it reports how many days the CA has left), and it
   refuses if the applied configuration carries a different CA.

   --webhook-config is what makes "the CA the cluster trusts" checkable:
   --ca-cert on its own only names a file. It may be omitted when a rendered
   configuration sits next to --ca-cert or --out-dir; when neither holds one —
   which is the case for a fresh rotation directory — the command refuses
   rather than issue a leaf under an unchecked CA.

   kubelet refreshes the mounted Secret in place, and the webhook re-reads
   tls.crt/tls.key when it changes; no restart is needed. Material that does not
   parse, has expired, drops a Service name from the SAN, or names a different
   issuer than the certificate the process started with is logged and does not
   replace what is serving. The server refuses to start on an expired
   certificate and logs from 30 days before notAfter
   (serving_cert_expiring_total).

   The issuer check is a pin on the issuer name and key id, not chain
   verification — the webhook cannot link a certificate parser. It is what
   stops a Secret from some other installation being applied here: that leaf
   parses and has not expired, and the API server, which trusts one caBundle,
   would reject every handshake made with it. So step 5 — a new CA — needs the
   webhook Pods restarted, not just the Secret replaced.

   A swap onto material that is already inside the 30-day window, replacing
   material that was not, is an old Secret being re-applied; the webhook undoes
   it and keeps serving what it had (serving_cert_rollbacks_total). Rotate with
   more than 30 days of --days.

   `ferrumctl lint-deploy <dir>` (FD022) checks a directory that holds both the
   rendered configuration and the Secret: the caBundle must be the CA that
   signed the leaf, and neither may be expired or inside that 30-day window.

5. When the CA itself runs out, step 4 is not enough: re-run step 1 into a clean
   directory and re-apply steps 2 and 3, in that order. Applying a new caBundle
   before its Secret leaves the API server trusting an issuer nothing serves.
